{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_jqnezrs3a5cimkaqdhvkmexeg4kfowylny6wti4pacyj3oxytoqa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four phases are complete and independently verified.\n\n## Phase A — both programs build\n\n- **C:** `cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .` → `c_src/build/driver`\n- **Rust:** `cd translation && cargo build --release` → `translation/target/release/driver`\n\nBoth compile clean, no errors or warnings.\n\n## Phase B/C — what the C actually branches on\n\nThe whole program is `float x = 0.f; scanf(\"%f\", &x); driver(x);`, where `driver` hexdumps the 4 raw bytes. Since **`scanf`'s return value is ignored**, every input class comes from `%f`, and a failure is silent — `x` keeps `+0.f` and the exit status is still 0. I enumerated: EOF, whitespace-only, decimal/dot/exponent forms, hex forms, `inf`/`infinity`, `nan(...)`, truncated prefixes, overflow/underflow, and every rounding boundary.\n\n`tests/differential.rs` (32 tests) spawns **both binaries as subprocesses** and compares stdout, stderr and exit status byte for byte across ~50,000 inputs — including exhaustive enumeration of all strings of length ≤3 over the float alphabet, all length-4 strings over a core alphabet, all 256 single bytes, all 256 second bytes after 9 different prefixes, and exact decimal midpoints between adjacent `f32` values.\n\n## The bug I found and fixed\n\n**`-0x` printed `00000080` (−0.0) in Rust but `00000000` (+0.0) in C.**\n\nThe Rust code modelled `%f` as `strtof` does — take the longest valid prefix, so `-0x` falls back to `-0` = −0.0. And `strtof(\"-0x\")` genuinely does return −0.0. But glibc's `scanf` rejects a buffer that is nothing but an optionally-signed `0x` as a **matching failure**, so `x` is never written and the typed sign is silently dropped. The boundary is exact: `-0xg`/`-0xp` fail, but `-0x.` converts to −0.0 (the `.` gets appended to the buffer). Fixed with a `state == St::HexPrefix → None` check; `signed_bare_hex_prefix_loses_its_sign` pins both sides.\n\nWorth flagging two C behaviours that look contradictory but are both real, and are now locked down by tests: a truncated **exponent** backs out and keeps the mantissa (`1e` → 1.0, `0x1p` → 1.0), whereas a truncated **`infinity`** does *not* fall back to `inf` (`infi` → matching failure, 0.0). I preserved that asymmetry rather than normalising it.\n\n`translation/ERRORS.md` records the mismatch with its cause plus the preserved quirks.\n\n## Phase D — completion gate\n\n- ✅ Both programs build with no errors\n- ✅ All enumerated inputs give identical stdout, stderr, exit status\n- ✅ `cargo test` passes (32 tests, both debug and release)\n- ✅ No test ignored, skipped or disabled (`grep` for `#[ignore]`/`should_panic` finds nothing)\n- ✅ `c_src/` unmodified (`git status --porcelain c_src` empty)\n- ✅ No `#[no_mangle]`, `cdylib`, or `libloading` — the binary is driven exactly as a shell would"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":486,"cache_read_input_tokens":70952,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":486,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b8aa8f22-75ff-4aba-83a3-b041fffb6073","uuid":"61595116-20c7-4d8f-933c-28fc4c8b1b41","timestamp":"2026-08-28T16:29:29.440Z"}
